&lt;p&gt;# Comments: 0&lt;/p&gt;</description><guid isPermaLink="false">https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50070784</guid><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 12:43:33 +0000</pubDate></item><item><title>Show HN: MemorySync – Memory for AI Agents</title><link>https://memorysync.io/try</link><description>&lt;p&gt;I created MemorySync because I kept running into the problem of AI agents not being able to remember useful things about users between conversations. I wanted an easy way to give the agents memory in my application without having to build the whole thing myself.
MemorySync is an API for giving AI agents memory. The application sends MemorySync information provided by the user, MemorySync saves useful memories for that user, and they can be retrieved later when needed.
Give it a try without creating an account: &lt;a href="https://memorysync.io/try" rel="nofollow"&gt;https://memorysync.io/try&lt;/a&gt;
Store a memory, refresh the page, and ask for it back to see if it remembers.
If you're interested in using the API, I put the documentation here: &lt;a href="https://docs.memorysync.io/" rel="nofollow"&gt;https://docs.memorysync.io/&lt;/a&gt;
It's hosted only at the moment, so there is no self-hosting option. It's currently available in one US region, and it takes a few seconds to store a memory.
The free plan allows 10k saves and 1k searches per month, and no credit card is required.
I'd like to know if you think this is a useful abstraction for building AI agents, and what else you would expect from a memory API.&lt;/p&gt;
